  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nToday...Upper level low centered over the western FL peninsula\nwill maintain deep moisture over the area with PWATs climbing\n2.0-2.1\". Scattered showers and isolated storms over the Atlc this\nmorning will cross portions of the coast in light onshore flow.\nThe Atlc sea breeze will develop and push inland during the day.\nSo higher pops initially along the coast will transition inland\nwith the sea breeze. Rain chances look highest along the coast\nsouth of the Cape and over the interior (50-70%) with the lowest\nrain chances along the Volusia coast (20-40%). Cool temperatures \naloft (-8C at 500 mb) will support potential for a few strong\nstorms containing frequent lightning strikes, gusty wind up to 50\nmph, small hail, and locally heavy rainfall of 1-3 inches. Focus\nfor showers and storms will shift back over the Atlc waters\ntonight with some activity brushing the coast. Seasonably warm \ntemperatures with maxes in the upper 80s/near 90 with peak heat\nindices in the mid to upper 90s. \n\nMon-Sat...The upper level low off the west coast of Florida to \nstart off the week will slowly fill and lift northeast becoming \nabsorbed by a northern stream trough by mid week. This northern \nstream trough is forecast to cut-off near the mid Atlc states Thu-\nFri which should force a frontal boundary south across the area \nThu and south of the area Fri. Locally, deep moisture will remain \nthrough mid week, supporting high rain and storm chances, with a \nhigh (60-70 percent) chance of rain through Wed. Slightly drier \nair will filter across the local area into late week behind a \nfrontal passage resulting in rain chances becoming near normal, \n(30-50 percent), Friday into the weekend. Main storm hazards will \ncontinue to be frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds around 45 \nmph, and locally heavy rainfall. Seasonable temperatures are \nforecast with highs in the upper 80s to low 90s through mid week \nthen lowering a tad into the mid and upper 80s behind the front\nFri-Sat.",
